<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · site</p><h1 class="enc-title">Kizil Caves</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Kizil Caves are a complex of more than 230 Buddhist rock-cut caves near Kucha in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/xinjiang">Xinjiang</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a>, carved into cliffs above the Muzat River between about the 3rd and 8th centuries. Their wall paintings of the Buddha's lives, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jataka-tales">Jataka tales</a> and donors are the finest surviving art of the kingdom of Kucha. German expeditions removed many murals in the early 20th century, and some are now in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/berlin">Berlin</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/kizil-caves.jpg" alt="Kizil Caves" width="320" height="112" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Hiroki Ogawa · CC BY 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Kizil_sky.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
